{
  "park": "Prospect Park, Brooklyn, New York City",
  "overview": "Prospect Park opened in 1867. Designed by Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux, who also designed Central Park, it was conceived as a rural retreat for a growing city. [Olmsted Network](https://olmsted.org/olmsted-trail/jobs/prospect-park/)",
  "history": [
    {
      "period": "1776: Revolutionary War",
      "details": "Land that later became Prospect Park was part of the battlefield during the Battle of Brooklyn, also called the Battle of Long Island. Battle Pass preserves that connection. [NYC Parks historical guide](https://www.nycgovparks.org/sub_things_to_do/facilities/images/Brooklyn_Queens_Greenway_Guide.pdf)"
    },
    {
      "period": "1860s: Planning",
      "details": "Brooklyn was still an independent city. Civic leader James S. T. Stranahan championed a major public park, initially planned by engineer Egbert Viele. Calvert Vaux proposed a revised layout and brought Frederick Law Olmsted into the project. [National Park Service](https://www.nps.gov/places/prospect-park.htm)"
    },
    {
      "period": "1866–1873: Construction and opening",
      "details": "Construction began on July 1, 1866, and visitors entered the unfinished park in October 1867. Most of the original plan was completed by 1873, when a financial crisis slowed further work. [NYC Landmarks Preservation Commission](https://s-media.nyc.gov/agencies/lpc/lp/0903.pdf)"
    },
    {
      "period": "The original design",
      "details": "Olmsted and Vaux shaped woodland and farmland into sweeping meadows, winding paths, a wooded Ravine, and an engineered system of streams, waterfalls, and a lake. These carefully constructed landscapes created the experience of escaping into the countryside. [Olmsted Network](https://olmsted.org/olmsted-trail/jobs/prospect-park/)"
    },
    {
      "period": "Late 1800s–early 1900s: Monuments and architecture",
      "details": "Later designers added more formal entrances, monuments, and buildings, including the Boathouse. These additions introduced grand architectural features into the original pastoral landscape. [The Cultural Landscape Foundation](https://www.tclf.org/landscapes/prospect-park)"
    },
    {
      "period": "1930s–1960s: Expanded recreation",
      "details": "Under Parks Commissioner Robert Moses, the park gained or expanded playgrounds, sports facilities, the zoo, a bandshell, and a skating rink. These projects increased recreational opportunities while altering parts of the earlier design. [The Cultural Landscape Foundation](https://www.tclf.org/landscapes/prospect-park)"
    },
    {
      "period": "1970s–present: Decline and restoration",
      "details": "Neglect and New York City's fiscal crisis left landscapes and buildings deteriorating. Restoration accelerated after Tupper Thomas became park administrator in 1980. The Prospect Park Alliance, founded in 1987, partnered with the city to restore woodlands, waterways, historic structures, and visitor facilities. [Olmsted Network](https://olmsted.org/olmsted-trail/jobs/prospect-park/)"
    },
    {
      "period": "2018: Permanently car-free",
      "details": "Prospect Park became permanently car-free in 2018, another major change in how people experience its roads and landscape. [NYC Department of Transportation](https://www.nyc.gov/html/dot/html/about/grandarmyplaza.shtml)"
    }
  ]
}

