String username = request.getParameter("username");

// This is vulnerable to SQL injection:
// Input ' OR '1'='1 changes the query to match every user.
String sql = "SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = '" + username + "'";

try (java.sql.Statement stmt = connection.createStatement();
     java.sql.ResultSet results = stmt.executeQuery(sql)) {
    response.getWriter().println(results.next() ? "Found" : "Not found");
}
```
***
```java
// GOOD: Bind the request parameter using a PreparedStatement.
String username = request.getParameter("username");
String sql = "SELECT id FROM users WHERE username = ?";

try (java.sql.PreparedStatement stmt = connection.prepareStatement(sql)) {
    // The supplied username is treated as data, preventing SQL injection.
    stmt.setString(1, username);

    try (java.sql.ResultSet results = stmt.executeQuery()) {
        response.getWriter().println(results.next() ? "Found" : "Not found");
    }
}
```

Parameter binding keeps user input separate from SQL syntax. [Oracle JDBC documentation](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/jdbc/basics/prepared.html)

